# Target C-II — loaded-unit shell wedges collapse to sibling leaves or switches

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Status:** proof-only structural refinement; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** canonical full-core near-disk C-II branch; numerical corollaries use the proof-only minimax atomic gap `0.0021` and audited §171  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The loaded-unit near-diameter-switch theorem leaves a nominal “shell-wedge” branch:
a unit loaded-hull edge `AB` whose endpoints share an actual diameter neighbor `Z`.
That terminology hides a stronger fact.  Unless both endpoints are actual diameter
leaves with unique neighbor `Z`, the same four-point argument already produces an
inactive near-diameter switch.  The genuinely switch-free residue is therefore a
matching of **sibling diameter leaves**, not a regular-shell-like diameter cycle.

This matters because every such leaf endpoint must carry another positive contact.
Loaded-chord elimination then forces that extra contact either to make the endpoint an
exact double-unit hull corner or to run from the loaded leaf into an unloaded core
vertex.  Combining this with the boundary-atom ledger eliminates the abstract
loaded-unit count completely.

Use the following notation.

- `e_BB` is the number of loaded-loaded unit contacts.  Canonical loaded-chord
  elimination identifies these with exact unit loaded-hull sides.
- `M_BB` is a maximum matching in that unit-side subgraph.
- `C_11` is the number of loaded hull vertices whose two adjacent hull sides both have
  length exactly one.
- `N_sw` is the number of distinct inactive source pairs selected below, each of length
  at least `D-epsilon_D`.
- `E_leaf` is a set of distinct loaded-to-unloaded unit contacts selected at pure
  sibling-leaf endpoints which are not in `C_11`.
- `N_2` is the genuine two-edge outer-tooth count.

The near-switch scale is the explicit finite quantity from the preceding theorem,

\[
\eta_D=3^{1/4}\sqrt D,
\qquad
L_D=\sqrt{D\eta_D+\eta_D^2},
\qquad
\varepsilon_D={4L_D+1\over D}=O(D^{-1/4}).
\tag{SL.1}
\]

## 1. Every non-pure shell edge already gives a switch

Let `AB` be an edge of `M_BB`.

The preceding loaded-unit theorem proves that if `A` and `B` have no common actual
diameter neighbor, then one can select an inactive source pair of length at least

\[
D-\varepsilon_D.
\tag{SL.2}
\]

Now suppose instead that they share an actual diameter neighbor `Z`:

\[
|A-Z|=|B-Z|=D.
\tag{SL.3}
\]

Assume `A` has another actual diameter neighbor `X\ne Z`.  Apply the same four-point
argument to the two actual diameter edges

\[
AX,
\qquad BZ.
\]

Their opposite cross-pairs are `AZ` and `BX`.  The pair `AZ` is already an actual
diameter edge.  If `BX` were also an actual diameter edge, then

\[
A-X-B-Z-A
\]

would be a four-cycle in the actual diameter graph.  Canonical Woodall topology
excludes an even cycle.  Hence `BX` is inactive and, by the same midpoint/Ptolemy
estimate,

\[
|B-X|\ge D-\varepsilon_D.
\tag{SL.4}
\]

The same argument applies if `B` has a second diameter neighbor.

Therefore a matching edge can fail to produce an inactive near-diameter switch only
if

\[
\boxed{
\deg_H(A)=\deg_H(B)=1
\quad\text{and}\quad
N_H(A)=N_H(B)=\{Z\}.
}
\tag{SL.5}
\]

Call such an edge a **pure sibling wedge**.  Its two endpoints are genuine sibling
leaves of the actual Woodall diameter graph.

This is strictly narrower than the regular odd-shell regression.  In a regular odd
shell every diameter vertex has degree two, so its boundary unit edges are not pure
sibling wedges and fall on the switch-producing side of (SL.5).

## 2. Multiplicity of selected switches

Partition the matching

\[
M_{BB}=M_{sib}\sqcup M_{sw},
\]

where `M_sib` consists of pure sibling wedges and every edge of `M_sw` supplies one
inactive near-diameter pair by Section 1.

One selected inactive pair can be supplied by at most two matching edges.  Indeed,
every selected pair contains at least one endpoint of its generating matching edge,
while distinct edges of `M_BB` have disjoint endpoint sets.  The same unordered pair
can therefore be charged only once through each of its two endpoints.

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
N_{sw}\ge {|M_{sw}|\over2}.
}
\tag{SL.6}
\]

Every pair counted by `N_sw` is strictly inactive at the finite source and satisfies
(SL.2).

## 3. A pure sibling leaf needs another contact

Fix a pure sibling wedge `AB` with common diameter neighbor `Z`.  At endpoint `A`,
the actual diameter graph has degree one.  In the relative-interior full-core KKT
stress, the diameter reaction at `A` is therefore a positive multiple of `A-Z`.

If `AB` were the only contact incident with `A`, KKT equilibrium would force

\[
\alpha_{AB}(A-B)=\beta_{AZ}(A-Z)
\tag{SL.7}
\]

with both coefficients positive.  But the two vectors are not parallel.  Indeed, if
`A,B,Z` were collinear with `|A-B|=1` and `|A-Z|=|B-Z|=D`, then the two latter
distances would differ by one.  Thus (SL.7) is impossible.

Hence every pure sibling endpoint has at least one further unit contact.

Now take a pure sibling endpoint `A` which is **not** counted by `C_11`.  Any further
contact from `A` to another loaded vertex would, by canonical loaded-chord elimination,
be a loaded hull side.  Since `AB` is already one of the two hull sides incident with
`A`, this would make both adjacent hull sides unit and put `A` in `C_11`, contrary to
assumption.

Therefore every pure sibling endpoint outside `C_11` has a contact to an unloaded
positive-core vertex.  Choose one such contact.  Choices at distinct loaded endpoints
are distinct physical edges, so if `E_leaf` denotes the resulting selected edge set,

\[
\boxed{
2|M_{sib}|\le C_{11}+|E_{leaf}|.
}
\tag{SL.8}
\]

This uses no KKT coefficient floor.  It uses positivity only to rule out a one-contact
loaded leaf.

## 4. Eliminate the shell-wedge count

From (SL.6),

\[
4N_{sw}\ge2|M_{sw}|.
\tag{SL.9}
\]

Adding this to (SL.8) gives

\[
C_{11}+|E_{leaf}|+4N_{sw}
\ge2|M_{sib}|+2|M_{sw}|
=2|M_{BB}|.
\tag{SL.10}
\]

The exact loaded-unit hull-side graph is a subgraph of one cycle.  A union of path
components has matching number at least half its edge count, while the only possible
loss for a cycle is one edge when the full cycle is odd.  Hence

\[
2|M_{BB}|\ge e_{BB}-1.
\tag{SL.11}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
C_{11}+|E_{leaf}|+4N_{sw}\ge e_{BB}-1.
}
\tag{SL.12}
\]

The old loaded-unit branch is now replaced by three actual finite structures:

double-unit corners, loaded-to-unloaded leaf contacts, or inactive near-diameter
switches.

## 5. Combine with the exact boundary-atom ledger

The boundary-atom theorem gives the finite full-core inequality

\[
N_2+2e_{BB}\ge3q-b.
\tag{SL.13}
\]

Equation (SL.12) gives

\[
e_{BB}\le C_{11}+|E_{leaf}|+4N_{sw}+1.
\]

Substitute this upper bound into the left side of (SL.13).  Since the substituted
quantity is no smaller than `e_BB`,

\[
\boxed{
N_2+2C_{11}+2|E_{leaf}|+8N_{sw}
\ge3q-b-2.
}
\tag{SL.14}
\]

This is the main finite theorem.  It contains no metric long-side currency and no
abstract shell-wedge count.

## 6. Low-`t` normalized form with the `0.0021` atomic gap

On a convergent Type-II subsequence write

\[
t={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\qquad
\delta={b-P_F\over D}.
\]

Then

\[
{q\over D}=t+\kappa,
\qquad
{b\over D}=\pi+\delta+o(1).
\tag{SL.15}
\]

The hostile-audited minimax atomic gap `0.0021` gives

\[
C_{21}:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0021,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\]

and audited §171 gives the low-`t` squeeze

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}\le R_{21}(t),
\qquad
R_{21}(t):={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_{21}.
\tag{SL.16}
\]

Divide (SL.14) by `D`.  Its right side is

\[
3t+3\kappa-\pi-\delta-o(1).
\]

At fixed `t`, the minimum of `3\kappa-\delta` under (SL.16) occurs at
`\kappa=0`, `\delta=R_{21}(t)/2`.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {N_2+2C_{11}+2|E_{leaf}|+8N_{sw}\over D}
\ge {3t\over4}+{C_{21}\over2}.
}
\tag{SL.17}
\]

The cancellation of `pi` in (SL.17) is exact.

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {1\over D}
\max\{N_2,C_{11},|E_{leaf}|,N_{sw}\}
\ge {\frac34t+\frac12C_{21}\over13}.
}
\tag{SL.18}
\]

The denominator `13` is the sum of the coefficients `1+2+2+8` in (SL.14).

At the current proof-only floor

\[
t_{21}={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right)+0.0017279
=1.82124143797944\ldots,
\]

one obtains

\[
{3t_{21}\over4}+{C_{21}\over2}
=2.31929388534854\ldots,
\tag{SL.19}
\]

and hence

\[
\boxed{
\max\{N_2,C_{11},|E_{leaf}|,N_{sw}\}
\ge0.178407221949887\ldots D-o(D).
}
\tag{SL.20}
\]

The decimal is orientation only; (SL.17)--(SL.18) are the theorem.

## 7. Diameter-topology consequence of the pure sibling subbranch

Let `ell_H` be the number of degree-one vertices of the actual diameter graph and put

\[
\Xi_H:=\sum_{v:\deg_H(v)\ge3}(\deg_H(v)-2).
\]

Every pure sibling matching edge contributes two distinct diameter leaves, so

\[
\ell_H\ge2|M_{sib}|.
\tag{SL.21}
\]

Woodall topology gives the exact degree identity

\[
\boxed{
\Xi_H=\ell_H-2d_H.
}
\tag{SL.22}
\]

Indeed, in the forest case `d_H` is the number of nontrivial tree components and the
usual tree degree identity gives `ell_H=2d_H+Xi_H`; in the connected unicyclic case
`d_H=0` and the signed degree sum gives `ell_H=Xi_H`.

Thus if the switch count is negligible and most of `M_BB` lies in the pure sibling
class, the diameter graph is forced to carry a linear branching-excess population.
This is a genuine caterpillar/odd-unicyclic restriction, not a generic shell cycle.

At the current endpoint the loaded-unit matching floor is

\[
{|M_{BB}|\over D}
\ge {t_{21}\over8}+{C_{21}\over12}-o(1)
=0.386548980891423\ldots-o(1).
\]

If at least half of that matching is pure sibling, then

\[
{\ell_H\over D}\ge0.386548980891423\ldots-o(1).
\]

Using `d_H\le k` and `\kappa\le2R_{21}(t_{21})=0.0113511...`, (SL.22) gives

\[
{\Xi_H\over D}\ge0.363846780891425\ldots-o(1).
\tag{SL.23}
\]

This does not yet contradict Woodall: large-degree star centers can absorb linear
branching excess.  It does rule out interpreting the pure-sibling residue as a nearly
2-regular regular-shell diameter graph.

## 8. Scope audit

- All diameter edges used above are actual edges of the restored full diameter support.
- The near-switch estimate is the finite midpoint/Ptolemy estimate, not a qualitative
  limiting replacement of an inactive pair by an active pair.
- The even-four-cycle exclusion is used only for actual diameter edges.
- Loaded-chord elimination is used only in its canonical full-core near-disk scope.
- The KKT argument in Section 3 uses the relative-interior full-core stress so every
  incident actual contact and diameter edge has positive coefficient.
- `E_leaf` counts physical contact edges and has no occurrence multiplicity.
- Contact-isolated source points remain in the full source.
- The numerical low-`t` constants inherit the proof-only `0.0021` minimax-gap
  lifecycle.
- No exact unit equilateral triangle or strict same-cardinality sub-`D` competitor is
  produced.

**Disposition:** proof-only structural theorem.  C-II and Level I remain open.
